[
    {
        "content": "<p>Given two simple A-modules V W, I can see several ways of saying that V and W are non-isomorphic, namely <code>[IsEmpty (V ≃ₗ[A] W)]</code>, <code>[Subsingleton (V →ₗ[A] W)]</code> and explicit <code>(h : ∀ f : V →ₗ[A] W, f = 0)</code>.  Which one is preferred?</p>",
        "id": 632492194,
        "sender_full_name": "Jiaxi Mo",
        "timestamp": 1792223436
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><code>Subsingleton (V →ₗ[A] W)</code> does not say that, consider when <code>V = A = W = Unit</code></p>",
        "id": 632493213,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1792223741
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>So I think the only spelling that works is <code>[IsEmpty (V ≃ₗ[A] W)]</code></p>",
        "id": 632493274,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1792223764
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>We have <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/RingTheory/SimpleModule/Basic.html#IsSimpleModule.nontrivial\">IsSimpleModule.nontrivial</a>, so <code>V = A = W = Unit</code> is not simple by definition. I am also away from the case <code>A = Unit</code>.</p>",
        "id": 632496443,
        "sender_full_name": "Jiaxi Mo",
        "timestamp": 1792224724
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I am not sure that it's a good idea to attempt to answer the question \"how to canonically say two simple modules are not isomorphic\" with an answer that doesn't generalise to all modules. This feels like you're digging yourself into an unnecessary hole.</p>",
        "id": 632513397,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1792230352
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Whoops, I totally missed the word \"simple\" in your message, thanks for the correction!</p>",
        "id": 632615677,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1792264734
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"110038\">Kevin Buzzard</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/How.20to.20say.20non-isomorphic.3F/near/632513397\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>an answer that doesn't generalise to all modules</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Following on this, I'd vote for <code>[IsEmpty (V ≃ₗ[A] W)]</code> since this pattern easily generalizes to group isomorphism, ring isomorphism, homeomorphism, diffeomorphism, etc. etc.</p>",
        "id": 632618966,
        "sender_full_name": "Jack McCarthy",
        "timestamp": 1792265821
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Is it important (or even desirable?) that this argument is a typeclass instance argument?</p>",
        "id": 632909767,
        "sender_full_name": "Johan Commelin",
        "timestamp": 1792392914
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>For the isomorphic counterpart, both <code>{f : M →ₗ[R] N} (h : f ≠ 0) </code> and <code>Nonempty (N ≃ₗ[R] S)</code> have been used in Mathlib.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">bijective_of_ne_zero</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsSimpleModule</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsSimpleModule</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">N</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→ₗ</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">N</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">Function</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Bijective</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">linearEquiv_of_ne_zero</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsSemisimpleModule</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsSimpleModule</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">N</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→ₗ</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">N</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Submodule</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nonempty</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">N</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≃ₗ</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I think we should add a lemma like this <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/45019\">#45019</a> first:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">isEmpty_linearEquiv_iff_forall_linearMap_eq_zero</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsSimpleModule</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsSimpleModule</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">N</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">IsEmpty</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≃ₗ</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">N</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→ₗ</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">N</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 632928528,
        "sender_full_name": "Jiaxi Mo",
        "timestamp": 1792397717
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Or perhaps <code>IsEmpty (M ≃ₗ[R] N) ↔ Subsingleton (M →ₗ[R] N)</code></p>",
        "id": 633077042,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1792430816
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"112680\">Johan Commelin</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/How.20to.20say.20non-isomorphic.3F/near/632909767\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Is it important (or even desirable?) that this argument is a typeclass instance argument?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Good point. If we don't want to write it as an instance, maybe something like <code>(h : ¬ ∃ i : M ≃ₗ[R] N)</code>? Although this feels a bit clumsy</p>",
        "id": 633086526,
        "sender_full_name": "Jack McCarthy",
        "timestamp": 1792432613
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think it would be silly not to reuse <code>IsEmpty</code></p>",
        "id": 633097028,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1792434808
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>But you could reasonably require <code>(h : IsEmpty _)</code> instead of <code>[IsEmpty _]</code> if you argue the instance is never going to exist</p>",
        "id": 633097154,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1792434834
    }
]